window.GITA_TOTAL=701;
window.GITA_VERSES=[{"id": "1-2", "order": 2, "ch": 1, "v": 2, "chName": "Arjuna Vishada Yoga", "speaker": "सञ्जय उवाच", "sanskrit": ["दृष्ट्वा तु पाण्डवानीकं व्यूढं दुर्योधनस्तदा ।", "आचार्यमुपसङ्गम्य राजा वचनमब्रवीत् ॥"], "translit": ["dṛiṣhṭvā tu pāṇḍavānīkaṁ vyūḍhaṁ duryodhanastadā", "āchāryamupasaṅgamya rājā vachanamabravīt"], "theme": "When Duryodhana Saw the Pandava Army", "icon": "🏹", "storyIcon": "🐦", "hindi": "संजय ने कहा: पाण्डवों की सेना को व्यूह के रूप में खड़ी देखकर राजा दुर्योधन उसी समय अपने आचार्य द्रोण के पास गया और उनसे यह बात कही।", "english": "Sanjay said: Seeing the Pandava army standing in battle formation, King Duryodhana went at once to his teacher, Drona, and spoke these words.", "deeper": ["This verse is like the opening shot of a film. Sanjay is describing what he sees on the field of Kurukshetra. Duryodhana looks at the well-arranged Pandava army, and something stirs inside him. He does not stay calm and still. He walks quickly to his teacher.", "Notice that he is called <b>rājā</b>, a king. A king is meant to be steady and sure of himself. Yet here the king is the one who hurries over to speak. Many teachers read this as a gentle hint that, deep down, Duryodhana felt uneasy. What we <i>see</i> can change what we <i>feel</i>, and what we feel decides what we <i>do</i>.", "There is also a lesson in whom he went to. Duryodhana turned to his <b>āchārya</b>, his teacher. Turning to someone wiser when we feel unsure is a good habit. The Gita will keep returning to this idea: when the mind is shaken, we need a guide, and the best guide is wisdom."], "words": [["सञ्जय उवाच", "sañjaya uvācha", "Sanjay said"], ["दृष्ट्वा", "dṛiṣhṭvā", "having seen"], ["तु", "tu", "indeed / then"], ["पाण्डवानीकम्", "pāṇḍavānīkam", "the army of the Pandavas"], ["व्यूढम्", "vyūḍham", "arranged in battle formation"], ["दुर्योधनः", "duryodhanaḥ", "Duryodhana"], ["तदा", "tadā", "at that time"], ["आचार्यम्", "āchāryam", "to the teacher (Drona)"], ["उपसङ्गम्य", "upasaṅgamya", "having approached"], ["राजा", "rājā", "the king"], ["वचनम्", "vachanam", "words"], ["अब्रवीत्", "abravīt", "spoke"]], "background": ["In the very first verse (1.1), the blind king Dhritarashtra asks his minister Sanjay what his sons and the sons of Pandu did when they gathered on the holy field of Kurukshetra, ready to fight. Sanjay had been given the gift of seeing distant events, so he could describe the battlefield from far away.", "This verse is the start of Sanjay’s answer. The first thing he tells the king is about Dhritarashtra’s own eldest son, Duryodhana. Duryodhana has just looked over the Pandava army and goes to speak to Drona, the teacher who trained both the Kauravas and the Pandavas."], "lessons": [["Our eyes can change our feelings", "What we see around us, like a strong rival, can quickly make us feel worried or excited."], ["Notice your feelings before you act", "Duryodhana rushed off right away; pausing for a breath first is wiser."], ["Turn to your teacher", "When we feel unsure, asking a teacher or elder for guidance is a good thing to do."], ["Preparation matters", "The Pandava army stood in neat formation, and being well organised gives strength and calm."], ["A leader should be steady", "Being a king or captain means keeping calm so that others feel safe."]], "examples": [["📝", "Seeing the topper’s notes", "Aarav sees his classmate’s neat, colourful notes before the exam and suddenly feels nervous. He could panic, or he could calmly ask his teacher how to prepare better."], ["🏏", "The other team looks strong", "Before a cricket match, Kavya’s team watches the opposing team warm up with fancy kits. She takes a deep breath and tells her friends, “Let’s just play our best.”"], ["🙋", "Going to the teacher", "Meera feels confused about a maths problem. Instead of hiding it, she walks up to her teacher after class and asks for help."], ["🎤", "Stage fright", "Rohan peeks through the curtain and sees the whole school hall full of people. His heart beats fast, so he finds his drama teacher, who helps him feel steady."], ["🧹", "Being well prepared", "Ananya’s group sorts out their science project the night before, with every task given out. On the day they feel calm because everyone knows their role."], ["🚲", "A bigger rival", "Imran notices a new boy in the cycle race who seems very fast. He feels a flutter in his stomach but reminds himself to focus on his own pace."], ["👑", "The class monitor’s worry", "Sneha is class monitor and feels worried when the class gets noisy. She stops, thinks, and then asks the teacher how best to handle it, rather than shouting."], ["🪔", "Noticing a feeling", "Before the Diwali rangoli contest, Tara notices her hands trembling when she sees others’ designs. Naming the feeling, “I’m nervous”, helps her calm down."]], "story": {"title": "The Bird’s Eye and the Teacher’s Test", "paras": ["In the Mahabharata, the teacher Drona trained the young princes of the Kuru family, the Pandavas and the Kauravas together. One day he wanted to see how well each of them could focus. He placed a wooden bird high up in a tree.", "He called the princes one by one and asked each to aim an arrow at the bird’s eye. Before they shot, Drona asked, “What do you see?” Some answered that they saw the tree, the branches, the bird, and even their friends and teacher standing nearby. Drona told them to step aside, because they were not ready.", "Then it was Arjuna’s turn. When Drona asked what he saw, Arjuna said, “Only the eye of the bird.” Drona asked again, and again Arjuna said he saw nothing else. Drona was very pleased and told him to shoot. Arjuna’s arrow flew straight to the target.", "Seeing too many things pulls the mind in many directions. In Gita 1.2, Duryodhana’s mind is busy looking at the other army, and it makes him restless. Arjuna’s calm focus in the bird test shows how steady a mind can be when it knows where to look."], "moral": "Lesson: A calm mind that stays on its goal is stronger than a mind that keeps getting stirred up by what it sees around it."}, "quiz": [{"q": "Who is speaking in this verse?", "o": ["Sanjay", "Arjuna", "Duryodhana", "Krishna"], "a": 0, "x": "Sanjay is narrating to King Dhritarashtra what is happening at Kurukshetra."}, {"q": "What did Duryodhana see before going to the teacher?", "o": ["A river in flood", "The Pandava army in formation", "A flying chariot", "His own tent"], "a": 1, "x": "He saw the Pandava army arranged in battle formation and then went to Drona."}, {"q": "Who is the āchārya (teacher) Duryodhana approached?", "o": ["Bhishma", "Vyasa", "Drona", "Krishna"], "a": 2, "x": "Drona was the teacher of both the Kauravas and the Pandavas."}, {"q": "What gentle lesson can we take from this verse?", "o": ["Always run away from rivals", "Notice your feelings and seek guidance when unsure", "Never talk to teachers", "Ignore all preparation"], "a": 1, "x": "What we see affects how we feel, so it helps to pause and turn to a wise guide."}]}, {"id": "1-3", "order": 3, "ch": 1, "v": 3, "chName": "Arjuna Vishada Yoga", "speaker": null, "sanskrit": ["पश्यैतां पाण्डुपुत्राणामाचार्य महतीं चमूम् ।", "व्यूढां द्रुपदपुत्रेण तव शिष्येण धीमता ॥"], "translit": ["paśhyaitāṁ pāṇḍu-putrāṇām āchārya mahatīṁ chamūm", "vyūḍhāṁ drupada-putreṇa tava śhiṣhyeṇa dhīmatā"], "theme": "Pointing at the Other Side’s Strength", "icon": "🪖", "storyIcon": "🔥", "hindi": "दुर्योधन द्रोणाचार्य से कहता है: “हे आचार्य! पाण्डवों की इस विशाल सेना को देखिए, जिसे आपके ही बुद्धिमान शिष्य, द्रुपद के पुत्र धृष्टद्युम्न ने युद्ध के लिए सजाया है।”", "english": "Duryodhana says to his teacher Drona, “Look, O teacher, at this mighty army of the sons of Pandu. It has been arranged for battle by your own clever student, the son of Drupada.”", "deeper": ["Duryodhana is talking to Drona, the teacher of both the Pandavas and the Kauravas. He asks Drona to <i>look</i> at the enemy army. It sounds like a simple report, but he is also pointing out that the Pandava army is large and well organised.", "Notice the words “your own student.” Dhrishtadyumna, the Pandava commander, had learned from Drona himself. Many teachers and commentators read a small sting in this. Duryodhana seems to be saying, “The one leading our enemies learned from you. Will you really fight him with full strength?” He calls Dhrishtadyumna <i>dhīmān</i>, “clever,” which can be praise, but here it also sounds like a worry he is trying to hide.", "The lesson for us is gentle. When we are nervous, we may talk a lot, point at others, or try to push responsibility onto someone else. A calm heart simply looks at the situation honestly. This verse shows us a mind that is already uneasy, and that is where Arjuna’s own struggle later begins."], "words": [["पश्य", "paśhya", "look, behold"], ["एताम्", "etām", "this"], ["पाण्डुपुत्राणाम्", "pāṇḍu-putrāṇām", "of the sons of Pandu"], ["आचार्य", "āchārya", "O teacher"], ["महतीम्", "mahatīm", "mighty, great"], ["चमूम्", "chamūm", "army"], ["व्यूढाम्", "vyūḍhām", "arranged in battle formation"], ["द्रुपदपुत्रेण", "drupada-putreṇa", "by the son of Drupada"], ["तव", "tava", "your"], ["शिष्येण", "śhiṣhyeṇa", "by the student"], ["धीमता", "dhīmatā", "intelligent, wise, clever"]], "background": ["The Gita begins with the blind king Dhritarashtra asking his minister Sanjaya what his sons and the Pandavas did on the field of Kurukshetra (1.1). Sanjaya, who could see the whole battlefield from far away, begins to describe it.", "In the previous verse, Sanjaya says that Duryodhana looked at the Pandava army standing in formation. He went straight to his teacher Drona and began to speak (1.2). This verse is the first thing Duryodhana says."], "lessons": [["Look carefully before you act", "Duryodhana studies the other army, and honest observation is always a good first step."], ["Nervous people often talk a lot", "Duryodhana’s words show worry hiding behind a confident report."], ["Respect your teacher’s teaching", "A good teacher shares knowledge with everyone who learns sincerely, even if they later stand on the other side."], ["Prepare well", "Dhrishtadyumna arranged the Pandava army with skill, and good results begin with good planning."], ["Choose your words wisely", "Even a short sentence can carry praise, worry, or a hidden hint, so think about what you really mean."]], "examples": [["📚", "Exam week", "Aarav sees his classmate Nisha revising neatly with colour-coded notes. Instead of panicking, he looks honestly at how much he has prepared and makes a plan for the rest."], ["🏏", "Cricket match", "Before the match, Kabir tells his captain, “Look how tall their bowlers are!” He is really saying he feels nervous. The captain smiles and says, “Let’s practise our shots.”"], ["🗣️", "Hidden worry", "Riya keeps saying, “The other team has the best singer.” Her friend notices she is actually afraid of singing on stage. Naming that fear helps more than talking about the other team."], ["👩‍🏫", "Same teacher", "Two students from different schools learned dance from the same teacher. At the contest, each wonders if the teacher will be fair. The teacher says, “I taught you both with the same love.”"], ["🧩", "Group project", "Manav sees that another group has made a very neat chart. He admires the work and asks himself, “How can we make ours better?” instead of feeling jealous."], ["🎯", "Carrying out a plan", "Sana’s team wins the kabaddi match because her captain planned each player’s position. She learns that a good plan matters as much as strength."], ["🙏", "Respect for the teacher", "After a competition, Dev thanks his coach even though the winning player also trained with the same coach. He knows a teacher’s gift belongs to everyone."], ["😟", "Pointing outward", "When Tara forgets her homework, she says, “Everyone else’s bag is heavier than mine.” Her mother gently asks, “What is really bothering you?” Tara admits she is afraid of being scolded."]], "story": {"title": "The Teacher Who Taught His Destined Rival", "paras": ["Long ago, Drona and King Drupada studied together as friends in the same ashram. Later, Drupada became king of Panchala. When poor Drona came to him for help, remembering their old friendship, Drupada insulted him and did not honour his earlier promise.", "Drona later became the teacher of the Kuru princes. As his fee, he asked them to bring Drupada to him, and they did. Drona took half of Drupada’s kingdom. Hurt and angry, Drupada performed a great fire ceremony and prayed for a son who could defeat Drona.", "From that fire, the Mahabharata tells us, came Dhrishtadyumna, along with his sister Draupadi. Though Drona knew of Drupada’s wish, he still accepted Dhrishtadyumna as a student and taught him the art of warfare. A teacher’s duty was to teach, not to hold back knowledge out of anger.", "Years later, Dhrishtadyumna became the commander of the Pandava army. When Duryodhana says “your own student” in this verse, he is reminding Drona of this old history. It shows how worried he is, and how differently Drona, a true teacher, could see things."], "moral": "Lesson: A true teacher shares knowledge fairly, and a worried mind should look at the truth instead of hiding behind clever words."}, "quiz": [{"q": "Who is speaking in this verse?", "o": ["Arjuna", "Duryodhana", "Sanjaya", "Krishna"], "a": 1, "x": "Duryodhana is speaking to his teacher Drona."}, {"q": "Who arranged the Pandava army in battle formation?", "o": ["Dhrishtadyumna, son of Drupada", "Bhima", "Drona", "Dhritarashtra"], "a": 0, "x": "Drupada’s son Dhrishtadyumna, who was Drona’s student, arranged the army."}, {"q": "Why does Duryodhana call Dhrishtadyumna “your student”?", "o": ["To thank Drona", "To point out that Drona himself taught the enemy commander", "Because he was Duryodhana’s brother", "Because he wanted to make peace"], "a": 1, "x": "It reminds Drona that his own student leads the other side, hinting at Duryodhana’s worry."}, {"q": "What can we learn from Duryodhana’s way of speaking?", "o": ["Worry may hide behind many words", "Always blame the teacher", "Never look at the other team", "Fear is a sign of strength"], "a": 0, "x": "His words sound confident, but they show an uneasy mind."}]}, {"id": "1-4", "order": 4, "ch": 1, "v": 4, "chName": "Arjuna Vishada Yoga", "speaker": null, "sanskrit": ["अत्र शूरा महेष्वासा भीमार्जुनसमा युधि ।", "युयुधानो विराटश्च द्रुपदश्च महारथः ॥"], "translit": ["atra śhūrā maheṣhvāsā bhīmārjuna-samā yudhi", "yuyudhāno virāṭaśhcha drupadaśhcha mahā-rathaḥ"], "theme": "Counting Others’ Strength When You’re Nervous", "icon": "🏹", "storyIcon": "🕊️", "hindi": "दुर्योधन द्रोणाचार्य से कहता है: इस सेना में बड़े-बड़े धनुषधारी वीर हैं, जो युद्ध में भीम और अर्जुन के समान हैं। उनमें युयुधान (सात्यकि), राजा विराट और महारथी द्रुपद भी हैं।", "english": "Duryodhana continues to Drona: “In this army are mighty archers, equal in battle to Bhima and Arjuna. Among them are Yuyudhana, Virata, and Drupada, the great chariot-warrior.”", "deeper": ["Duryodhana has just asked Drona to look at the Pandava army. Now he begins to name its heroes one by one. On the surface it sounds like a simple report. But notice <i>what</i> he is doing: he is counting the strength of the other side. When we feel unsure inside, our mind often starts counting the strengths of whoever we are about to face.", "The names matter too. Yuyudhana (also called Satyaki) was a brave warrior and a close friend of Krishna. Virata was the king who sheltered the Pandavas during their hidden year. Drupada was the father of Draupadi, the Pandavas’ wife. These were not hired soldiers; they were friends and relatives who stood by the Pandavas. The verse quietly shows that the Pandavas were strong because they had <b>loyal friends</b>, not only because of their own skill.", "For us, the verse is a gentle mirror. It is good to look honestly at what lies ahead of us. But if we only stare at others’ strengths and forget our own preparation, fear grows. The Gita will slowly teach Arjuna, and us, how to meet a hard moment with a steady mind. This verse shows us how a restless, worried mind behaves before that teaching begins."], "words": [["अत्र", "atra", "here, in this army"], ["शूराः", "śhūrāḥ", "brave heroes"], ["महेष्वासाः", "maheṣhvāsāḥ", "mighty archers (great bow-wielders)"], ["भीमार्जुनसमाः", "bhīmārjuna-samāḥ", "equal to Bhima and Arjuna"], ["युधि", "yudhi", "in battle"], ["युयुधानः", "yuyudhānaḥ", "Yuyudhana (Satyaki)"], ["विराटः", "virāṭaḥ", "Virata, the king of Matsya"], ["च", "cha", "and"], ["द्रुपदः", "drupadaḥ", "Drupada, king of Panchala"], ["च", "cha", "and"], ["महारथः", "mahā-rathaḥ", "a great chariot-warrior"]], "background": ["The Gita begins with the blind king Dhritarashtra asking Sanjaya what his sons and the Pandavas did on the field of Kurukshetra (1.1). Sanjaya describes how Duryodhana, seeing the Pandava army arranged for battle, went straight to his teacher Drona (1.2).", "In the previous verse, Duryodhana pointed to the great Pandava army, arranged by Drupada’s son, who was once Drona’s own student (1.3). Now, in this verse, he begins to list the famous warriors standing in that army."], "lessons": [["Look honestly at what you face", "It is wise to know the strengths of a challenge, but do not let it shrink your courage."], ["Fear often shows up as counting", "When we feel nervous, we may keep listing reasons why the other side is strong."], ["Good friends are real strength", "The Pandava army was powerful because loyal friends and family stood with them."], ["Notice other people’s talents", "Seeing what others do well is a good habit when it leads to respect and learning, not worry."], ["Prepare and then trust yourself", "Hard moments feel lighter when we have practised and have good people beside us."]], "examples": [["🏏", "Before the cricket match", "Aarav saw the other school’s team practising and began listing how good each player was. His coach said, “Good, you have noticed. Now let’s also remember how well we have practised.”"], ["📚", "The class topper", "Meera kept thinking about how smart her classmates were before the Maths Olympiad. She felt calmer when she opened her notebook and saw how many problems she had already solved."], ["🤝", "Friends who show up", "When Rohan’s family moved to a new city, his old friends called him every week. He realised that having people who stand by you is a kind of strength that no marks can measure."], ["🏃", "Relay race", "Divya’s relay team was nervous about a faster team. They stopped comparing and practised their baton passing again and again. Teamwork was their biggest strength."], ["🎤", "Talking fast when scared", "Kabir talked non-stop about how tough his rivals were before the quiz. His mother smiled and said, “When we are nervous, we talk a lot. Take a deep breath first.”"], ["🎨", "Admiring a friend’s art", "Sana saw how beautifully her friend Tara drew. Instead of feeling small, she asked Tara for tips and improved her own drawing."], ["🏸", "Badminton tournament", "Ishaan checked the draw sheet and saw strong names in his group. His father said, “It is fine to know who you will play. Now go and play your best game.”"], ["🙏", "Thanking the helpers", "Before her dance show, Lakshmi thanked her teacher, her mother who stitched the costume, and her friends who rehearsed with her. She felt that many people were standing behind her."]], "story": {"title": "The Pigeons and the Net", "paras": ["In an old Panchatantra tale, a flock of pigeons, led by their wise king Chitragriva, saw some grains scattered under a tree. They landed to eat, but a hunter had hidden a net there. In a moment, all the pigeons were caught.", "Some pigeons began to panic. Each one pulled in a different direction, and the net only grew tighter. Chitragriva said calmly, “If we pull separately, we are weak. Let us all rise together at the same moment.”", "The pigeons lifted off together, carrying the whole net into the sky. The hunter ran after them but could not follow. Chitragriva led them to his friend Hiranyaka, a little mouse, who gnawed through the ropes one by one until every pigeon was free.", "The pigeons were not strong alone. Their strength came from staying calm, acting together, and having a true friend to help them."], "moral": "Lesson: Real strength comes from unity and good friends, and a calm mind can find a way out of any net."}, "quiz": [{"q": "What is Duryodhana doing in this verse?", "o": ["Naming the brave warriors in the Pandava army", "Asking Drona to leave the battlefield", "Praising his own soldiers", "Blowing a conch shell"], "a": 0, "x": "He is pointing out the great heroes standing on the Pandava side."}, {"q": "Which warriors does the verse say are equal to Bhima and Arjuna in battle?", "o": ["Only the Kaurava princes", "Mighty archers such as Yuyudhana, Virata and Drupada", "Drona’s students from the ashram", "The charioteers of the Kauravas"], "a": 1, "x": "The verse names Yuyudhana, Virata and Drupada among the mighty archers and great warriors."}, {"q": "What does the long list of Pandava heroes quietly show?", "o": ["The Pandavas had no friends", "The Pandavas were strong because loyal friends stood with them", "The battle was already over", "Drona was afraid of them"], "a": 1, "x": "Allies like Virata and Drupada were friends and relatives who supported the Pandavas."}, {"q": "What can happen when we only keep counting the strengths of others?", "o": ["We become stronger at once", "We win without trying", "Our fear can grow and we may forget our own preparation", "We stop having friends"], "a": 2, "x": "Looking honestly is wise, but staring only at others’ strength can feed worry."}]}, {"id": "1-5", "order": 5, "ch": 1, "v": 5, "chName": "Arjuna Vishada Yoga", "speaker": null, "sanskrit": ["धृष्टकेतुश्चेकितानः काशिराजश्च वीर्यवान् ।", "पुरुजित्कुन्तिभोजश्च शैब्यश्च नरपुङ्गवः ॥"], "translit": ["dhṛiṣhṭaketuśhchekitānaḥ kāśhirājaśhcha vīryavān", "purujit kuntibhojaśhcha śhaibyaśhcha nara-puṅgavaḥ", "yudhāmanyuśhcha vikrānta uttamaujāśhcha vīryavān"], "theme": "Naming the Brave: Duryodhana Counts Heroes", "icon": "🛡️", "storyIcon": "🏏", "hindi": "दुर्योधन द्रोणाचार्य से कहता है कि पांडवों की सेना में धृष्टकेतु, चेकितान, पराक्रमी काशिराज, पुरुजित्, कुन्तिभोज और मनुष्यों में श्रेष्ठ शैब्य जैसे वीर भी हैं।", "english": "Duryodhana goes on pointing out the Pandava warriors to Drona: Dhrishtaketu, Chekitana, the mighty king of Kashi, Purujit, Kuntibhoja, and Shaibya, the best among men.", "deeper": ["This verse is simply a list of names, but a list can tell us how someone feels. Duryodhana is not calmly chatting. He is busy counting the strong people on the other side, one by one. When we feel unsure inside, we often count the strengths of others again and again.", "The names also show how wide the Pandava side was. Kuntibhoja was the king who had raised Kunti, the Pandavas’ mother, as his own daughter. Shaibya was a king connected to Yudhishthira’s family by marriage. Kashi was a famous kingdom. Many good people had come to stand with the Pandavas, and Duryodhana could see it.", "The gentle lesson is this: <b>it is wise to know what you are facing, but not wise to let worry take over your heart.</b> <i>Preparation</i> makes us steady. <i>Fear</i> only makes us restless. Keep this in mind as we read on, because Arjuna’s own heart will soon be shaken in a very different way."], "words": [["धृष्टकेतुः", "dhṛiṣhṭaketuḥ", "Dhrishtaketu, a brave king of Chedi"], ["चेकितानः", "chekitānaḥ", "Chekitana, a famous warrior"], ["काशिराजः", "kāśhirājaḥ", "the king of Kashi"], ["च", "cha", "and"], ["वीर्यवान्", "vīryavān", "mighty, full of strength and courage"], ["पुरुजित्", "purujit", "Purujit, a warrior king"], ["कुन्तिभोजः", "kuntibhojaḥ", "Kuntibhoja, the king who raised Kunti"], ["शैब्यः", "śhaibyaḥ", "Shaibya, a respected king"], ["नरपुङ्गवः", "nara-puṅgavaḥ", "best among men (literally, ‘bull among men’)"]], "background": ["The great war of Kurukshetra is about to begin. Blind King Dhritarashtra has asked Sanjay what his sons and the Pandavas are doing on the battlefield (1.1). Sanjay tells him that Duryodhana saw the Pandava army arranged for battle and went straight to his teacher Drona (1.2).", "Duryodhana points out the Pandava army and reminds Drona that it was arranged by Drupada’s son, Drona’s own student (1.3). He then begins naming great archers and chariot-warriors such as Yuyudhana, Virata and Drupada (1.4). In this verse (1.5), he keeps adding more names to the list."], "lessons": [["Know your situation", "It is sensible to look carefully at the challenge in front of you."], ["Don’t let worry grow", "Counting only other people’s strengths can make our own heart shaky."], ["Good people gather together", "Many kind and brave people stood with the Pandavas, which shows how friendship and goodness attract support."], ["Respect every strength", "Seeing and naming what is good in others is a fine habit, even when they are on the other team."], ["Prepare, then stay steady", "Preparation helps us be calm; fear without preparation only makes us restless."]], "examples": [["📝", "Before the exam", "Aarav kept checking how many marks his classmates usually got, and he forgot to revise. His mother said, “Know the test, but study your own pages.” He closed the list and opened his book."], ["🏏", "The other cricket team", "Before a gully cricket match, Rohan noticed how many good batters the other team had. He told his friends, “Let’s practise our bowling instead of just worrying.”"], ["🎤", "Singing competition", "Ananya heard that the girl before her had a beautiful voice. She took three slow breaths and thought about her own song. She sang calmly and enjoyed it."], ["🏊", "Swimming race", "Kabir looked at the swimmers in the next lanes and felt his stomach tighten. His coach said, “Look at the water in your own lane.” That helped him begin well."], ["🎨", "Drawing contest", "Meera saw that other children had brought fancy colour boxes. She felt small for a moment. Then she remembered how much she had practised and began her drawing happily."], ["♟️", "Chess match", "Before a school chess match, Sameer read about his opponent’s past wins. He learned a few useful things, then stopped reading and played his own game."], ["🤝", "Group project", "Priya listed the strengths of her teammates: Zoya draws well, Dev speaks well. Naming their strengths made her feel happy to be part of the team, not afraid."], ["🏃", "Sports day", "Tara saw how tall and fast the runners from the next school were. She felt nervous but whispered to herself, “I’ve trained, and I’ll do my best.” She ran with a smile."]], "story": {"title": "Nisha and the Quiz Team List", "paras": ["Nisha was captain of her school’s quiz team in Pune. The day before the inter-school quiz, she got a paper listing the other teams. She began reading the names aloud: “This boy won last year. That girl knows all the science. This team has three champions!”", "With each name, her voice grew smaller and her hands felt colder. Her friend Sanjana said, “Nisha, you have counted all of them twice. Have you counted us even once?”", "Nisha stopped. Then she slowly said, “Sanjana remembers every date in history. Imran is quick with maths. Kavya never forgets a capital city.” As she named her own team, her shoulders relaxed. She still respected the other teams, but she was no longer shaking.", "The next day, they did not win every round, but they answered bravely and helped each other. Walking home, Nisha smiled and said, “Knowing the other teams was useful, but practising together was better.”"], "moral": "Lesson: Look at the challenge honestly, but spend more time preparing and trusting your own team than counting everyone else’s strengths."}, "quiz": [{"q": "What is Duryodhana doing in this verse?", "o": ["Naming more brave warriors on the Pandava side", "Asking Drona for blessings", "Running away from the battlefield", "Telling a bedtime story"], "a": 0, "x": "He keeps pointing out the strong warriors in the Pandava army to Drona."}, {"q": "What does ‘nara-puṅgavaḥ’ mean?", "o": ["Fastest runner", "Best among men", "King of birds", "Youngest prince"], "a": 1, "x": "Nara-puṅgava literally means ‘bull among men’, a way of saying the best of men."}, {"q": "Who was Kuntibhoja?", "o": ["Duryodhana’s teacher", "A demon king", "The king who raised Kunti as his daughter", "Arjuna’s charioteer"], "a": 2, "x": "Kuntibhoja adopted and raised Kunti, the mother of the Pandavas."}, {"q": "What gentle lesson can we take from this list of names?", "o": ["Never look at your challenges", "Know the challenge, but don’t let worry take over", "Always avoid teamwork", "Only count other people’s strengths"], "a": 1, "x": "Being aware helps us prepare, but worry alone makes us restless."}]}, {"id": "1-6", "order": 6, "ch": 1, "v": 6, "chName": "Arjuna Vishada Yoga", "speaker": null, "sanskrit": ["युधामन्युश्च विक्रान्त उत्तमौजाश्च वीर्यवान् ।", "सौभद्रो द्रौपदेयाश्च सर्व एव महारथाः ॥"], "translit": ["saubhadro draupadeyāśhcha sarva eva mahā-rathāḥ"], "theme": "Every Name Counts on the Team", "icon": "🏹", "storyIcon": "🌀", "hindi": "दुर्योधन द्रोणाचार्य को पांडवों की सेना के और वीरों के नाम बताता है: पराक्रमी युधामन्यु, बलवान उत्तमौजा, सुभद्रा का पुत्र अभिमन्यु और द्रौपदी के पुत्र। ये सभी महारथी हैं।", "english": "Duryodhana keeps pointing out the Pandava warriors to Drona: brave Yudhamanyu, strong Uttamauja, Subhadra’s son Abhimanyu, and the sons of Draupadi. All of them, he says, are maharathas, great chariot-warriors.", "deeper": ["This verse is another part of Duryodhana’s list. He is naming the brave people on the other side, one after another. Notice that he does not call them weak. He calls every one of them <b>vikrānta</b> (brave), <b>vīryavān</b> (strong) and <b>mahāratha</b> (great warrior). Inside, Duryodhana is counting, and the count is making him nervous.", "The names in this verse include some very young people. Abhimanyu was Arjuna’s teenage son, and the sons of Draupadi were also young. A good army has experienced elders and fresh young fighters together. Everyone has a place, and a team is strong when every member, big or small, is counted.", "The gentle lesson for us is about how fear feels. When we are worried, we start counting the other side’s strengths again and again, and the worry grows. The Gita will slowly teach Arjuna, and us, to stay steady and look at our duty clearly, not just at the numbers."], "words": [["युधामन्युः", "yudhāmanyuḥ", "Yudhamanyu, a warrior prince of the Panchala side"], ["च", "cha", "and"], ["विक्रान्तः", "vikrāntaḥ", "brave, mighty"], ["उत्तमौजाः", "uttamaujāḥ", "Uttamauja, another Panchala warrior"], ["च", "cha", "and"], ["वीर्यवान्", "vīryavān", "full of strength"], ["सौभद्रः", "saubhadraḥ", "son of Subhadra, that is Abhimanyu"], ["द्रौपदेयाः", "draupadeyāḥ", "the sons of Draupadi"], ["च", "cha", "and"], ["सर्वे", "sarve", "all"], ["एव", "eva", "indeed"], ["महारथाः", "mahā-rathāḥ", "great chariot-warriors"]], "background": ["The great war of Kurukshetra is about to begin. Duryodhana goes to his teacher Drona and tells him to look at the Pandava army, which was arranged by Drona’s own clever student, the son of Drupada (1.3). He then names mighty archers who are equal to Bhima and Arjuna, such as Yuyudhana, Virata and Drupada (1.4).", "He keeps going with the list: Dhrishtaketu, Chekitana, the king of Kashi, Purujit, Kuntibhoja and Shaibya (1.5). In this verse (1.6) he adds Yudhamanyu, Uttamauja, Abhimanyu and the sons of Draupadi, and says all of them are maharathas."], "lessons": [["Every member matters", "A team is made of many people, young and old, and each one adds strength."], ["Young people can be brave too", "Abhimanyu and Draupadi’s sons were young, yet they were counted among great warriors."], ["Fear loves to count", "When we are worried, we keep listing the other side’s strengths and forget our own."], ["Give honest credit", "Even Duryodhana had to admit that the other side’s warriors were brave and strong."], ["Stay steady", "Numbers and names do not decide everything; a calm mind and clear duty matter more."]], "examples": [["⚽", "Everyone on the team", "Aarav is the youngest player in the school football team. His captain says, “We need you too.” Aarav feels proud because every player counts, not only the tall ones."], ["🎭", "Small role, big help", "In the Annual Day play, Meera only has to hold the curtain rope. Without her, the play cannot start. Every small job is part of the big success."], ["📝", "Counting rivals", "Before the quiz, Kabir keeps listing how many clever kids are in the other house. His stomach feels tight. He realises that counting rivals only makes him more scared, so he goes back to revising."], ["👏", "Giving credit", "Ananya loses the race to Priya. She still says, “You ran really fast!” Giving honest credit to someone else is a sign of a big heart."], ["🧒", "Young but capable", "Ten-year-old Rohan helps his grandmother use her new phone. Even though he is small, he is the expert in the house today. Age does not decide who can help."], ["🏏", "Practice shows", "Sara knows that the other cricket team practises every evening. Instead of worrying, she decides to practise her own bowling a bit longer. Noticing strength in others can push us to work harder."], ["🧹", "Class cleanup", "During Swachh Bharat week, every child in class 5B picks a small task: sweeping, watering plants, collecting paper. The school ground shines because nobody was left out."], ["👨‍👩‍👧", "Family team", "At Diwali, Dadi makes sweets, Papa hangs lights, Mummy draws rangoli, and little Ishaan arranges the diyas. The festival is beautiful because each person did a part."]], "story": {"title": "Abhimanyu and the Secret of the Chakravyuha", "paras": ["Abhimanyu was the son of Arjuna and Subhadra, and he is one of the warriors named in this verse. There is a well-known tale that, while he was still in his mother’s womb, his father was telling Subhadra how to break into a chakravyuha, a spinning circle-shaped battle formation.", "Arjuna explained how to enter the circle. But before he reached the part about how to come out, Subhadra fell asleep. So the baby Abhimanyu heard only half of the secret.", "Years later, on the thirteenth day of the Kurukshetra war, the Pandavas were challenged with this very formation. Young Abhimanyu bravely said that he knew how to enter it. He went in with great courage and fought like a true maharatha, but he did not know the way out, and he was lost in the battle.", "The Pandavas and their friends were deeply sad. People remember Abhimanyu even today for his courage at such a young age. They also remember how important it is to listen to a lesson all the way to the end."], "moral": "Lesson: Young people can be very brave, and we should learn things completely, listening until the end."}, "quiz": [{"q": "Who is Abhimanyu in this verse called by the name Saubhadra?", "o": ["Son of Subhadra", "Son of Draupadi", "Brother of Drona", "King of Kashi"], "a": 0, "x": "Saubhadra means the son of Subhadra, who is Abhimanyu."}, {"q": "What does the word mahā-rathāḥ mean?", "o": ["Great chariot-warriors", "Wise teachers", "Little children", "Royal cooks"], "a": 0, "x": "Mahā means great and rathī means chariot-warrior, so mahārathas are great warriors."}, {"q": "Who is Duryodhana speaking to while naming these warriors?", "o": ["His teacher Drona", "Arjuna", "Krishna", "Bhishma’s mother"], "a": 0, "x": "Duryodhana is pointing out the Pandava heroes to his teacher Drona."}, {"q": "What gentle lesson does this list of names teach us?", "o": ["Every team member counts, young or old", "Only the tallest person matters", "Never practise before a match", "Rivals are always weak"], "a": 0, "x": "The verse names young and old warriors alike, showing that every member adds to a team’s strength."}]}, {"id": "1-7", "order": 7, "ch": 1, "v": 7, "chName": "Arjuna Vishada Yoga", "speaker": null, "sanskrit": ["अस्माकं तु विशिष्टा ये तान्निबोध द्विजोत्तम ।", "नायका मम सैन्यस्य संज्ञार्थं तान्ब्रवीमि ते ॥"], "translit": ["asmākaṁ tu viśhiṣhṭā ye tānnibodha dwijottama", "nāyakā mama sainyasya sanjñārthaṁ tānbravīmi te"], "theme": "Now Look at Our Own Team", "icon": "🛡️", "storyIcon": "🏏", "hindi": "हे द्विजश्रेष्ठ (द्रोणाचार्य)! अब हमारी ओर जो विशेष योद्धा हैं, उन्हें भी जान लीजिए। आपकी जानकारी के लिए मैं अपनी सेना के उन नायकों के नाम बताता हूँ।", "english": "Duryodhana says: “O best of the twice-born, now also know the chief warriors on our side, the leaders of my army. I will name them for your information.”", "deeper": ["After describing the Pandava heroes in verses 1.4 to 1.6, Duryodhana now turns to his own side. The little word <i>tu</i> (“but” or “and now”) marks this turn. He has looked at the other army; now he looks at his own. Preparing for a big task means knowing both: what stands in front of you and what you have with you.", "He calls Drona <i>dvijottama</i>, “best of the twice-born”, a respectful title. He says he is naming his leaders <i>sanjñārtham</i>, “for your information”. Yet Drona already knows these warriors well. Many readers see a king who is a little anxious and keeps talking, hoping his teacher will feel reassured. Words spoken from worry sound different from words spoken from calm confidence.", "For us, the verse is a gentle lesson. It is good to know your team, to value each person’s strength, and to be clear about who is helping you. But a list of names cannot give peace of mind. Real strength comes from a clear heart and doing the right thing, and the Gita will teach us more about that as it goes on."], "words": [["अस्माकम्", "asmākam", "our, on our side"], ["तु", "tu", "but, and now"], ["विशिष्टाः", "viśhiṣhṭāḥ", "special, chief, most distinguished"], ["ये", "ye", "who"], ["तान्", "tān", "them"], ["निबोध", "nibodha", "know, take note of"], ["द्विजोत्तम", "dvijottama", "O best of the twice-born (Drona)"], ["नायकाः", "nāyakāḥ", "leaders, commanders"], ["मम", "mama", "my"], ["सैन्यस्य", "sainyasya", "of the army"], ["संज्ञार्थम्", "sanjñārtham", "for information, for your knowledge"], ["तान्", "tān", "them"], ["ब्रवीमि", "bravīmi", "I tell, I name"], ["ते", "te", "to you"]], "background": ["The great war of Kurukshetra is about to begin. Duryodhana, the Kaurava prince, has looked at the Pandava army standing in battle formation and has gone to his teacher Drona. He points out the mighty Pandava warriors one by one: Bhima, Arjuna, Yuyudhana, Virata, Drupada, and others (1.4–1.6), including Abhimanyu and the sons of Draupadi.", "Having named the strong warriors on the other side, Duryodhana now changes direction. In this verse he begins to speak about the leaders of his own army. The names themselves follow in the next verses (1.8 onwards)."], "lessons": [["Know your own team", "Before a big task, it helps to know who is with you and what each person can do."], ["Look at both sides", "Seeing the challenge is only half the work; seeing your own strengths and gaps is the other half."], ["Respect your teachers", "Duryodhana addresses Drona politely, and speaking respectfully to elders is always a good habit."], ["Notice worry in words", "When we talk too much to win approval, it may be because we feel unsure inside."], ["Strength is more than a list", "Names and numbers help, but a calm mind and a right purpose matter even more."]], "examples": [["🏏", "Cricket captain’s list", "Before the school match, Kabir’s captain reads out each player’s strength: Imran bowls fast and Neha fields well. Knowing his team makes him feel ready."], ["📚", "Group project roles", "Ananya’s group has a science project. She asks who draws well, who writes neatly and who speaks clearly. Everyone gets a job that suits them."], ["🧘", "Calm instead of nervous", "Rohan keeps telling his coach about his team’s good players before a race. Then he notices he is nervous. He takes three slow breaths and feels steadier."], ["🙏", "Speaking politely", "Meera asks her teacher, “Ma’am, may I explain our plan to you?” Her respectful tone makes the teacher happy to listen."], ["🎭", "Annual day preparation", "Before the school play, Arjun checks not only the other class’s costumes but also his own class’s dialogues. Then he rehearses his lines once more."], ["🏑", "Thanking teammates", "After the hockey match, Simran names every teammate and says what each one did well. The whole team smiles."], ["🧩", "Don’t only compare", "Dev keeps looking at how fast his cousin solves puzzles. His mother says, “Look at what you can do well too.” Dev then tries his own way and enjoys it."], ["🎒", "Packing for a trip", "Before a school trip, Tara checks the list: water bottle, cap, notebook. She does not only worry about the weather; she makes sure her own bag is ready."]], "story": {"title": "Meera and the Kabaddi Captain’s Speech", "paras": ["In a small town near Pune, Meera was made captain of her school kabaddi team. The next day they would play against the strongest team in the district. That evening Meera went to her coach, Sir, and began talking very fast.", "“Sir, their raider Kavya is very quick, and their defenders are tall,” she said. Then she added, “But now look at our team! Pooja is the best at tackles, Ishita is fast, and Sneha never gives up. Sir, let me tell you everyone’s strengths!” Coach listened kindly, though he already knew his players well.", "When she finished, he smiled and said, “Meera, I know your team. But I think you are saying all this to calm your own heart.” Meera blushed, then laughed. “Yes, Sir. I am a little afraid.” Coach said, “It is good to know your team. Now also trust them, and play honestly.”", "The next day Meera did not rattle off lists. She took a deep breath, looked at her friends, and said, “Let us play our best and play fair.” They played well. They did not win every raid, but they played together and went home proud."], "moral": "Lesson: Knowing your team’s strengths is wise, but a calm heart and trust in each other matter even more than a long list of names."}, "quiz": [{"q": "Whom is Duryodhana speaking to in this verse?", "o": ["Bhishma", "Drona", "Arjuna", "Sanjaya"], "a": 1, "x": "Duryodhana addresses his teacher Drona as “dvijottama”."}, {"q": "What does Duryodhana say he will do now?", "o": ["Name the leaders of his own army", "Run away from the battlefield", "Ask Drona to leave", "Blow the conch"], "a": 0, "x": "He says he will name the chief leaders of his own army “for your information”."}, {"q": "What does the little word “tu” (“but, and now”) show in this verse?", "o": ["He is feeling sleepy", "He turns from the other side to his own side", "He is asking a question", "He is ending the speech"], "a": 1, "x": "After describing the Pandava warriors, he now shifts attention to his own warriors."}, {"q": "Which everyday lesson fits this verse best?", "o": ["Ignore your team", "Only count the other team’s strengths", "Know your own team and stay calm", "Never speak to teachers"], "a": 2, "x": "The verse reminds us to know our own side, while a calm mind gives the deeper strength."}]}, {"id": "1-8", "order": 8, "ch": 1, "v": 8, "chName": "Arjuna Vishada Yoga", "speaker": null, "sanskrit": ["भवान्भीष्मश्च कर्णश्च कृपश्च समितिञ्जयः ।", "अश्वत्थामा विकर्णश्च सौमदत्तिस्तथैव च ॥"], "translit": ["bhavānbhīṣhmaśhcha karṇaśhcha kṛipaśhcha samitiñjayaḥ", "aśhvatthāmā vikarṇaśhcha saumadattis tathaiva cha"], "theme": "Naming Our Team, Counting Our Strength", "icon": "🛡️", "storyIcon": "🏹", "hindi": "दुर्योधन द्रोणाचार्य से कहता है: “आप स्वयं, भीष्म, कर्ण, कृपाचार्य, सदा युद्ध जीतने वाले, अश्वत्थामा, विकर्ण और सोमदत्त के पुत्र भूरिश्रवा, ये सब हमारी ओर के प्रमुख वीर हैं।”", "english": "Duryodhana tells Drona the names of the great warriors on his side: Drona himself, Bhishma, Karna, Kripa (always victorious in battle), Ashvatthama, Vikarna, and Saumadatti, the son of Somadatta.", "deeper": ["In this verse Duryodhana begins his list of heroes on his own side. The first name he says is <b>“bhavān”</b>, which means “you”. He is speaking to Drona, his teacher and commander, so he puts Drona first. It is polite, and it also shows that Duryodhana wants Drona to feel important and stay committed.", "Then come Bhishma, the respected grandsire; Karna, the great archer; Kripa, who is described as <i>samitiñjaya</i>, “victorious in battle”; Drona’s son Ashvatthama; Duryodhana’s own brother Vikarna; and Saumadatti, the son of Somadatta. A leader who lists his strengths this way is gathering courage. Naming what we have can make us feel steadier.", "But the verse also asks us to look a little deeper. A long list of famous names can sound strong, yet true strength also needs a good purpose and a calm heart. Duryodhana counts people, while Arjuna, in the next verses, will count something different: his own doubts and duty. The Gita will slowly teach us which kind of counting really matters."], "words": [["भवान्", "bhavān", "you (Your Honour, Drona)"], ["भीष्मः", "bhīṣmaḥ", "Bhishma, the grandsire"], ["च", "ca", "and"], ["कर्णः", "karṇaḥ", "Karna"], ["कृपः", "kṛpaḥ", "Kripa"], ["समितिञ्जयः", "samitiñjayaḥ", "ever victorious in battle"], ["अश्वत्थामा", "aśvatthāmā", "Ashvatthama, Drona’s son"], ["विकर्णः", "vikarṇaḥ", "Vikarna, Duryodhana’s brother"], ["सौमदत्तिः", "saumadattiḥ", "Saumadatti, son of Somadatta"], ["तथा", "tathā", "likewise"], ["एव", "eva", "indeed"]], "background": ["The two armies stand ready at Kurukshetra. Blind King Dhritarashtra asks Sanjaya what is happening (1.1). Seeing the Pandava army arranged for battle, Duryodhana goes to his teacher Drona and speaks (1.2). He points out the strong Pandava warriors one by one: Bhima, Arjuna, Yuyudhana, Virata, Drupada and others (1.4–1.6).", "Now, in 1.7, Duryodhana turns to his own side. He says, “Now also know the chief warriors on our side, the leaders of my army. I will name them for your information.” Verse 1.8 is the start of that list, and it begins with Drona himself."], "lessons": [["Know your strengths", "It helps to remember the good people and skills we have, especially before a big task."], ["Respect your teachers", "Duryodhana begins with “you”, Drona. Speaking respectfully to elders is a good habit."], ["Names are not enough", "A team full of famous players still needs the right purpose and a clear heart."], ["Listing can hide worry", "Sometimes we talk loudly about our strength because we feel nervous inside. It is good to notice that feeling honestly."], ["Teamwork has many kinds of people", "The list has elders, a brother, a friend and a teacher’s son. Every team is made of different relationships."]], "examples": [["🏏", "Picking the cricket team", "Rohan is captain of the lane cricket team. He names his best bowler, best batter and best fielder before the match. It makes the whole team feel ready."], ["🙏", "Respect first", "Before a school quiz, Meera thanks her teacher, Mrs. Iyer, for helping her practise. She remembers that her success has many helpers."], ["📝", "Counting what I know", "Before the maths test, Aarav lists the chapters he has learnt well. This calms his nervousness and shows him what still needs revision."], ["😬", "Talking big when scared", "Kabir keeps saying, “Our team is the best!” before the kabaddi final. Later he admits he was just feeling nervous, and his friend helps him relax."], ["👨‍👩‍👧‍👦", "A family team", "At Diwali, the whole family shares the work. Dadi plans the sweets, Papa hangs the lights, and Ananya draws the rangoli. Everyone has a part."], ["🎭", "School play", "Sneha makes a list of who will do the costumes, music and lines for the school play. Knowing each person’s strength makes planning easier."], ["🤝", "Famous is not the same as good", "Ishaan’s group has the best-known students, but they quarrel. Another group works together happily and finishes the project first."], ["🌱", "Brothers in one team", "Tara and her younger brother Dev are on the same relay team. She cheers for him by name, and he runs faster because she believes in him."]], "story": {"title": "Drona’s Test of the Wooden Bird", "paras": ["When the Kuru princes were learning archery from Guru Drona, he once wanted to test them. He placed a wooden bird high on a tree and asked each prince to aim at it. But first he asked each one a question: “Tell me what you see.”", "Yudhishthira said, “I see the tree, the branches, the bird, and you, my teacher, and my brothers.” Drona asked him to step aside. Several other princes also named many things around them, and each was asked to step aside.", "Then it was Arjuna’s turn. “I see only the eye of the bird,” he said. Drona smiled. Arjuna did not count the tree, the people or the sky. His mind was steady on his aim. When Drona told him to shoot, the arrow hit the target.", "On the battlefield, Duryodhana counts big names to feel strong. Drona, who taught the bird test, already knew that great names alone are not enough. A calm mind that knows what matters is also needed."], "moral": "Lesson: Counting strong names can give courage, but a steady mind and a clear purpose are what truly bring success."}, "quiz": [{"q": "Whom does Duryodhana name first in this verse?", "o": ["Karna", "Drona, whom he is speaking to", "Bhishma", "Ashvatthama"], "a": 1, "x": "He begins with “bhavān”, meaning “you”, which refers to Drona."}, {"q": "What does “samitiñjaya” describe in this verse?", "o": ["Someone always victorious in battle", "Someone who loves music", "Someone who is very old", "Someone who is a king"], "a": 0, "x": "Samitiñjaya means “victorious in battle” and is used here for Kripa."}, {"q": "Who is Ashvatthama?", "o": ["Duryodhana’s brother", "Bhishma’s teacher", "Drona’s son", "Karna’s charioteer"], "a": 2, "x": "Ashvatthama was the son of Drona and fought on the Kaurava side."}, {"q": "What honest lesson can we take from Duryodhana’s long list?", "o": ["Never trust a team", "Names help, but purpose and a steady mind matter more", "Only elders can win", "Lists are a waste of time"], "a": 1, "x": "Knowing our strengths is useful, but real strength also needs a good purpose and a calm heart."}]}, {"id": "1-9", "order": 9, "ch": 1, "v": 9, "chName": "Arjuna Vishada Yoga", "speaker": null, "sanskrit": ["अन्ये च बहवः शूरा मदर्थे त्यक्तजीविताः ।", "नानाशस्त्रप्रहरणाः सर्वे युद्धविशारदाः ॥"], "translit": ["anye cha bahavaḥ śhūrā madarthe tyaktajīvitāḥ", "nānā-śhastra-praharaṇāḥ sarve yuddha-viśhāradāḥ"], "theme": "Many Loyal Heroes Stand Beside Me", "icon": "🛡️", "storyIcon": "🏏", "hindi": "दुर्योधन कहता है: और भी बहुत से शूरवीर हैं, जिन्होंने मेरे लिए अपने जीवन की आशा छोड़ दी है। वे सब तरह-तरह के शस्त्र चलाने वाले हैं और युद्ध की कला में बहुत निपुण हैं।", "english": "Duryodhana tells Drona: “There are many other brave warriors too, who are ready to give up their lives for my sake. They carry many kinds of weapons, and all of them are skilled in battle.”", "deeper": ["After naming the famous warriors in verse 8, Duryodhana adds <i>“and many others.”</i> He wants Drona to feel that his army is not only big but also loyal, brave and well trained. Every word is meant to build confidence.", "Notice the words <b>“for my sake.”</b> Duryodhana speaks of others’ courage as if it belongs to him. A good leader is grateful for the people who stand by him and cares for them. Someone who only counts on them may be hiding his own worry.", "The verse also teaches that strength has three parts: <b>loyalty</b> (people who stand with you), <b>variety</b> (many kinds of weapons and skills), and <b>training</b> (being expert at what you do). Having all three is wonderful, but the Gita will soon show that even a strong army cannot give peace to a heart that is not right."], "words": [["अन्ये", "anye", "others"], ["च", "cha", "and, also"], ["बहवः", "bahavaḥ", "many"], ["शूराः", "śhūrāḥ", "heroes, brave warriors"], ["मदर्थे", "madarthe", "for my sake"], ["त्यक्तजीविताः", "tyakta-jīvitāḥ", "ready to give up their lives"], ["नानाशस्त्रप्रहरणाः", "nānā-śhastra-praharaṇāḥ", "armed with many kinds of weapons"], ["सर्वे", "sarve", "all of them"], ["युद्धविशारदाः", "yuddha-viśhāradāḥ", "skilled in warfare"]], "background": ["The two armies stand ready at Kurukshetra. Duryodhana, the Kaurava prince, goes to his teacher Drona and points out the Pandava army (1.2–1.3). He names their great warriors, such as Yudhamanyu, Uttamauja, Abhimanyu and the sons of Draupadi (1.6).", "Then he says he will name the leaders on his own side (1.7). In verse 8 he lists Drona, Bhishma, Karna, Kripa, Ashvatthama, Vikarna and Saumadatti. Now, in verse 9, he adds that many more warriors are ready to give up their lives for him."], "lessons": [["Be grateful for loyal people", "Those who stand by you, like family, friends and teachers, are a great blessing, so thank them."], ["Skills need practice", "Being “skilled in battle” comes from training, and every skill we admire comes from hard work."], ["Different strengths make a team", "Many kinds of weapons remind us that a team needs many kinds of talents."], ["Do not use people only for yourself", "“For my sake” is a warning: a good leader cares for his people and does not just count on them."], ["Numbers cannot calm a worried heart", "Duryodhana keeps counting his helpers, yet he still feels unsure, because real confidence comes from doing right."]], "examples": [["🏏", "Full team ready", "Before the match, Rohan’s captain looks around and smiles. Eleven players are ready, and a few more are waiting as substitutes. Knowing the team is ready makes everyone feel braver."], ["📚", "Skilled through practice", "Ananya is the best at maths quizzes because she solves two sums every evening. Skill does not come by luck. It comes from practice, just like the warriors who were “skilled in battle.”"], ["🙏", "Thanking those who help", "Meera’s parents wake up early every day to pack her lunch and get her to school. She decides to say “thank you” each morning. People who work for us deserve our gratitude."], ["🎨", "Many tools, one project", "For the school exhibition, one friend paints, one writes, and one builds the model. Everyone uses a different tool, and the project turns out wonderful."], ["🤝", "Friends who stay back", "Karan’s group project is not finished, and three friends stay after school to help. Karan says, “You are all so kind,” and does not treat their help as something he is owed."], ["😟", "Counting friends to feel brave", "Aarav is nervous about his stage speech, so he keeps counting how many friends are in the audience. Then his teacher tells him, “Be calm and prepare your heart, too.” True courage grows inside us."], ["💧", "A captain who cares", "Sana is the kabaddi captain. At practice she checks that every teammate has water and is not hurt. A good leader looks after the people who work with him."], ["🛠️", "Right tool for the job", "In Grandfather’s workshop, Dev learns that a hammer, a saw and a screwdriver each have a job. Having many tools helps only if you know how to use each one well."]], "story": {"title": "Captain Meera and the Village Cricket Team", "paras": ["In a small village near Nashik, eleven-year-old Meera was captain of the school cricket team. Before the big final, she stood in the field and looked at her team. Pranav bowled fast, Salma was a brilliant fielder, and Gopal could hit sixes. “With a team like this, we will win!” she thought proudly.", "Then Meera noticed something. Her friends had given up their games and holidays to practise with her for weeks. Meera had been saying “my team” all week, as if they were hers to command. Suddenly she felt a little embarrassed.", "She called everyone together. “Friends, you have all worked so hard and given up so much. This is not my team, it is our team. Win or lose, thank you for standing with me.” Everybody cheered, and the team felt warm and happy.", "They played with joy and lost by just two runs. But nobody cried. Pranav said, “We played well together,” and the whole team had ice-cream afterwards. Meera felt that day she had become a true captain."], "moral": "Lesson: Loyal friends are a gift, not a possession. Thank them, care for them, and remember that real strength is not only in counting people but in being fair and kind to them."}, "quiz": [{"q": "What does Duryodhana say about the many other warriors on his side?", "o": ["They are afraid of the battle", "They are ready to give up their lives for him", "They have no weapons", "They are not trained"], "a": 1, "x": "He says they are “madarthe tyaktajīvitāḥ,” ready to give up their lives for his sake."}, {"q": "What does “yuddha-viśhāradāḥ” mean?", "o": ["Fond of sleeping", "Skilled in warfare", "Afraid of fighting", "Teachers of children"], "a": 1, "x": "Viśhāradāḥ means skilled or expert, and yuddha means battle."}, {"q": "What does “nānā-śhastra” (many kinds of weapons) remind us about teams?", "o": ["Everyone should do the same thing", "A team needs many different skills", "Weapons are more important than people", "Only one person matters"], "a": 1, "x": "Different tools and talents together make a team strong."}, {"q": "What gentle warning is hidden in the words “for my sake”?", "o": ["Never ask for help", "A good leader should care for people, not just rely on them", "Leaders should be afraid", "Loyalty is not important"], "a": 1, "x": "Leaders should be grateful to those who stand by them and look after their well-being."}]}, {"id": "1-10", "order": 10, "ch": 1, "v": 10, "chName": "Arjuna Vishada Yoga", "speaker": null, "sanskrit": ["अपर्याप्तं तदस्माकं बलं भीष्माभिरक्षितम् ।", "पर्याप्तं त्विदमेतेषां बलं भीमाभिरक्षितम् ॥"], "translit": ["aparyāptaṁ tadasmākaṁ balaṁ bhīṣhmābhirakṣhitam", "paryāptaṁ tvidameteṣhāṁ balaṁ bhīmābhirakṣhitam"], "theme": "Loud Words, Hidden Worry", "icon": "🛡️", "storyIcon": "🏏", "hindi": "दुर्योधन कहता है: “भीष्म की रक्षा में हमारी सेना हमारे लिए बहुत बड़ी है (या: काफ़ी नहीं है), और भीम की रक्षा में उनकी सेना छोटी है (या: काफ़ी है)।” इन शब्दों के दो अर्थ हो सकते हैं, और दोनों में उसके मन की बेचैनी झलकती है।", "english": "Duryodhana says: “This army of ours, guarded by Bhishma, is unlimited (or, some read, not enough). That army of theirs, guarded by Bhima, is limited (or, some read, enough).” The word can be read two ways, and both readings show what is going on in his mind.", "deeper": ["The Sanskrit word <i>aparyāptam</i> can mean “unlimited, immeasurable” or “not enough, insufficient.” Great teachers have understood the verse in both ways. Either Duryodhana is boasting that his army is too big to count, or his worry is slipping out and he is admitting that Bhishma’s protection may not be enough.", "Notice what he is doing. He is counting soldiers and comparing sizes. He trusts numbers, big names and weapons. He does not ask whether his side is doing what is right. When we only measure strength and never ask about goodness, we often feel shaky inside, however loudly we talk.", "The verse teaches us to listen to our own words. Sometimes we speak very boldly because we are a little afraid. <b>Real confidence is quiet and comes from doing the right thing and preparing well.</b> Boasting is often a way to cover up fear."], "words": [["अपर्याप्तम्", "aparyāptam", "unlimited, immeasurable (or, in another reading, not enough)"], ["तत्", "tat", "that"], ["अस्माकम्", "asmākam", "our"], ["बलम्", "balam", "army, strength"], ["भीष्माभिरक्षितम्", "bhīṣhmābhirakṣhitam", "protected by Bhishma"], ["पर्याप्तम्", "paryāptam", "limited, measurable (or, in another reading, enough)"], ["तु", "tu", "but, whereas"], ["इदम्", "idam", "this"], ["एतेषाम्", "eteṣhām", "of these, their"], ["बलम्", "balam", "army, strength"], ["भीमाभिरक्षितम्", "bhīmābhirakṣhitam", "protected by Bhima"]], "background": ["On the field of Kurukshetra, two great armies stood facing each other, ready for the war. Duryodhana, the Kaurava prince, saw the Pandava army arranged in battle formation and went to his teacher Drona. He pointed out the Pandava warriors (1.2–1.6) and then named the great heroes on his own side (1.7–1.8).", "He then added that many other brave soldiers were ready to give up their lives for him, all skilled with many weapons (1.9). Now, in verse 10, he sums it all up. He compares the two armies, his own guarded by the elder Bhishma and the Pandava army guarded by the mighty Bhima."], "lessons": [["Listen to your own words", "Loud boasting can sometimes be a sign that we are feeling unsure inside."], ["Numbers are not everything", "A big team or a famous name does not guarantee that we are on the right path."], ["Don’t compare only to feel safe", "Counting other people’s strengths all the time can make us more anxious, not less."], ["Quiet confidence is stronger", "Honest effort and a clear heart give us a calm courage that shouting cannot give."], ["Words can have layers", "One sentence can carry both pride and fear, so we should look carefully at what we really mean."]], "examples": [["🏏", "Cricket match boast", "Rohan shouts, “Our team will crush them!” before the match, but he keeps glancing at the other team’s tall bowler. His loud words are covering a little nervousness."], ["📚", "Exam talk", "Priya tells everyone, “I don’t even need to study.” Inside, she is worried about the maths test. She feels better after she admits it and opens her book."], ["🎨", "Drawing contest", "Kabir counts how many prizes his rival has won instead of finishing his own picture. When he stops comparing and starts colouring, he feels calmer."], ["🎤", "Stage fright", "Ananya says, “Singing in front of everyone is nothing!” but her hands are cold. Her friend says, “It’s okay to be nervous,” and she relaxes."], ["⚽", "Football team size", "Imran’s team has more players than the other side, so the boys think they have already won. They forget to practise passing and lose the match."], ["🧒", "Big brother’s name", "Devika says, “My brother is the school captain, so nobody can beat me.” But the race is about her own running, not her brother’s fame."], ["🎲", "Board game bragging", "Sameer keeps saying, “I’m sure to win!” whenever he is actually behind. His grandmother smiles and says, “Play well and let the game speak.”"], ["🤝", "Honest friend", "Lakshmi says, “I’m a bit scared of the quiz, but I’ve prepared.” Her honesty makes her feel lighter, and she does better than the loud boasters."]], "story": {"title": "Meera and the Kabaddi Match", "paras": ["Meera was the captain of her school kabaddi team in a small town in Maharashtra. Before the big match against the neighbouring school, she stood in front of her team and said loudly, “We are the best! They have no chance at all!”", "But as the other team walked in, Meera went quiet. She began counting their tall players and their strong raiders. Her voice had been big, but her heart was thumping. She told herself that numbers decided everything.", "Her coach, Sunita Didi, noticed and took her aside. “Meera, you spoke very loudly just now. Are you worried?” Meera nodded. Didi said, “Then let’s say it plainly. We cannot control how tall they are. We can only control how well we play together.”", "Meera told her team the truth: “I’m a little nervous, and I think we all are. Let’s stay together and do what we practised.” The team smiled and relaxed. They played carefully, and whether they won or lost, they played with calm hearts and proud faces."], "moral": "Lesson: Loud boasting often hides worry. Honest effort and a calm heart give a much steadier kind of strength."}, "quiz": [{"q": "Who does Duryodhana say is protecting his army in this verse?", "o": ["Bhima", "Bhishma", "Drona", "Karna"], "a": 1, "x": "He says his army is guarded by Bhishma, while the Pandava army is guarded by Bhima."}, {"q": "What can the word aparyāptam mean?", "o": ["Very happy", "Unlimited or not enough", "Fast and quick", "Peaceful"], "a": 1, "x": "Aparyāptam can mean “unlimited” or “not enough,” so the verse can be read in two ways."}, {"q": "What does Duryodhana’s way of speaking show us?", "o": ["He trusts numbers and strength, and may be hiding some worry", "He is sleepy", "He wants peace", "He has no army"], "a": 0, "x": "He compares army strength and talks boldly, but his words also hint at inner unease."}, {"q": "What is a good thing to do when we feel nervous before a contest?", "o": ["Boast loudly so nobody notices", "Stay silent and run away", "Admit it honestly and focus on our preparation", "Count only the other team’s strengths"], "a": 2, "x": "Honesty about our feelings and good preparation give us calm, real confidence."}]}, {"id": "1-11", "order": 11, "ch": 1, "v": 11, "chName": "Arjuna Vishada Yoga", "speaker": null, "sanskrit": ["अयनेषु च सर्वेषु यथाभागमवस्थिताः ।", "भीष्ममेवाभिरक्षन्तु भवन्तः सर्व एव हि ॥"], "translit": ["ayaneṣhu cha sarveṣhu yathā-bhāgamavasthitāḥ", "bhīṣhmamevābhirakṣhantu bhavantaḥ sarva eva hi"], "theme": "Guard the Leader, But Do Your Own Job", "icon": "🛡️", "storyIcon": "🤼", "hindi": "दुर्योधन अपने सब सेनापतियों से कहता है: “आप सभी लोग सब मोर्चों पर अपने-अपने निश्चित स्थान पर डटे रहकर केवल भीष्म पितामह की ही सब ओर से रक्षा कीजिए।”", "english": "Duryodhana tells all his commanders: “Stand firm in your own places at every entrance and front, and be sure that all of you protect Bhishma above everything.”", "deeper": ["After counting his warriors, Duryodhana gives an order. Everyone must stay at their <b>assigned post</b>, and everyone must guard <b>Bhishma</b>. Bhishma was the commander-in-chief, the leader whom the whole army looked up to. A good army does need to protect its leader, and standing in the right place is a sensible plan.", "But listen to the feeling under the words. Duryodhana has just said that Bhishma guards the army (1.10). Now he asks the army to guard Bhishma. It sounds as if he is nervous. His whole hope rests on <i>one person</i>, and he is afraid of what will happen if that person is hurt. Fear often hides inside strong, loud orders.", "For us, the verse is a gentle lesson. Teamwork means every person stays at their post and does their part well. But strength that rests on one person alone is shaky. Real confidence comes from a good cause, honest effort and a team that trusts each other, not from fear of losing one star."], "words": [["अयनेषु", "ayaneṣu", "at the entrances, positions or fronts"], ["च", "ca", "and"], ["सर्वेषु", "sarveṣu", "in all"], ["यथाभागम्", "yathā-bhāgam", "according to each one’s assigned share or division"], ["अवस्थिताः", "avasthitāḥ", "standing firmly, stationed"], ["भीष्मम्", "bhīṣmam", "Bhishma"], ["एव", "eva", "only, above all"], ["अभिरक्षन्तु", "abhirakṣantu", "must protect on all sides"], ["भवन्तः", "bhavantaḥ", "you (respected ones)"], ["सर्वे", "sarve", "all"], ["एव", "eva", "indeed"], ["हि", "hi", "certainly"]], "background": ["The Kuru army and the Pandava army stand face to face at Kurukshetra. Duryodhana, the Kaurava prince, goes to his teacher Drona and describes the Pandava army (1.2). Then he names the great warriors on his own side: Drona, Bhishma, Karna, Kripa, Ashvatthama and others (1.8). He adds that many more brave fighters are ready to give their lives for him (1.9).", "Next he compares the two armies. Ours is guarded by Bhishma, theirs by Bhima (1.10). The words can be read as “unlimited or not enough” and “limited or enough,” so his confidence and his worry are mixed together. In this verse he turns from talking to ordering. Every commander must hold the assigned position and protect Bhishma."], "lessons": [["<b>Know your post</b>", "In a team, everyone has a place and a job, and the whole team gains when each person does theirs well."], ["<b>Protect what matters</b>", "It is wise to take care of your leaders, your helpers and your strengths."], ["<b>Don’t lean on one person only</b>", "If all your hope sits on a single person, the whole plan wobbles when that person is missing."], ["<b>Notice fear behind loud words</b>", "Sometimes strong orders come from a worried heart, so we should be gentle with ourselves and others when we feel afraid."], ["<b>Real strength is deeper</b>", "A good cause, honest effort and trust in each other give more safety than guarding one star."]], "examples": [["🏏", "Gully cricket", "Aarav’s team has one great batsman, Kabir. Everyone keeps saying “Don’t let Kabir get out!” but the team also needs fielders, bowlers and other batters who play well."], ["📚", "Group project", "Meera’s group has a topper, Ishaan. Instead of sharing the work, everyone leaves it all to him. A fair group gives each child a part to do."], ["🚌", "Class monitor duty", "On a school trip, Ritu is asked to count heads at the back of the bus and Dev at the front. Because each stays at their post, nobody is left behind."], ["😟", "Before a test", "Tanvi keeps telling her friend, “Please help me, only you can save me!” She is really afraid. Her real help is to revise a little herself each day."], ["🏑", "Hockey team", "Sandeep’s coach says, “Defenders, stay in your positions.” When every player holds their place, the team is safe even if the star forward is tired."], ["🍳", "Helping at home", "On Diwali, each cousin has a job: one makes rangoli, one arranges diyas and one serves sweets. Nobody tries to do everything, and nobody is idle."], ["🧒", "Leaning on a big brother", "Pranav always asks his elder brother to fight his battles with friends. Pranav grows braver when he learns to speak up kindly for himself."], ["🎭", "School play", "Everyone keeps worrying about the lead actor forgetting his lines. The director smiles and says, “Let’s all learn our own lines well, so the play stands on all of us.”"]], "story": {"title": "Vikram and the Kabaddi Match", "paras": ["In a village near Satara, the boys’ kabaddi team had reached the final. Their star raider was Vikram, quick as a sparrow. The captain, Sandeep, was nervous. Before the match he gathered everyone and said, “Forget everything else. Just protect Vikram. If Vikram is safe, we will win!”", "In the first half, every boy ran around Vikram like a wall. Nobody guarded the corners, and nobody raided on his own. The other team noticed this. They simply ignored Vikram and attacked the empty corners, and they scored point after point.", "At the break, Vikram said, “Sandeep bhaiya, I am happy that you care for me. But I cannot win alone. Let Pooja and Ravi guard the corners as you planned, and let Imran raid too. I will do my part and each of you will do yours.”", "Sandeep took a deep breath. His worry had made him forget the plan. In the second half everyone went back to their own positions and trusted one another. The team won by two points, and they all cheered for each other, not only for Vikram."], "moral": "Lesson: Take care of your leaders and stars, but a team wins when everyone stands firm at their own post and trusts each other, not when it is afraid of losing one person."}, "quiz": [{"q": "What does Duryodhana ask his commanders to do in this verse?", "o": ["Run away from the battlefield", "Stay at their posts and protect Bhishma", "Surrender to the Pandavas", "Go and ask Krishna for help"], "a": 1, "x": "He tells them to hold their assigned positions and guard Bhishma on all sides."}, {"q": "What does “yathā-bhāgam” suggest?", "o": ["Everyone doing whatever they like", "Everyone standing in their own assigned place", "Everyone sleeping in turns", "Everyone running to the front"], "a": 1, "x": "It means “according to each one’s share or division,” so each person keeps their own post."}, {"q": "What does Duryodhana’s order hint about his mind?", "o": ["He is completely calm", "He is bored", "He is worried and depends heavily on one person", "He is planning a picnic"], "a": 2, "x": "Asking everyone to guard Bhishma shows that he fears losing the one leader his hopes rest on."}, {"q": "What is the best lesson for us from this verse?", "o": ["Let one star do all the work", "Never protect anyone", "Do your own part well and don’t depend on one person alone", "Give orders loudly when afraid"], "a": 2, "x": "Teamwork means each person does their part, and a team is stronger when it doesn’t depend on a single star."}]}];
